 "evidence": "A calculation on committed products and published values: the sea-level equation with migrating shorelines (Farrell and Clark 1976; Mitrovica and Milne 2003) on LOLA at 16 px/deg with spherical harmonics to degree 359, the load Love numbers of geography's crustal-loading study (elastic, compressible, every degree) with the geology lane's viscoelastic change for the present Moon (degrees 2-50, incompressible) added, and the drainage network study's rims found on the deformed heights. The water arrives at once in the response; the creep's flow laws are Earth olivine's. Not validated against any observed loading on the Moon.",
 "reading_rule": "Levels and surfaces are on the deformed Moon's equipotential, in metres against the atlas's common level on the dry Moon's geoid (level_change_m) or the redrawn common level (surface_above_the_common_level_m). The common level holds 28% of the surface as the atlas does; the three upper seas stand 1 cm under their rims over all the land their water joins. Read the design case, the central profile 100 million years after the water arrives, as the settled surface the round builds on, barring erosion; the central and stiff billion-year cases as the most the present temperatures could add; the soft case as the warm mantle's risk; the elastic and 1,000-year cases as the first years and the first millennium. Sill depths are below the common level, four-connected 16 px/deg cells. The atlas case deforms nothing. Water beyond the atlas counts the seas and the lakes against the atlas's seas at its level and the same lakes.",
